+  <td>Zin-Azshari</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>Zin-Azshari adore</td><td>glory</td>
+</tr>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> <em>Zin</em> meaning "glory" is confirmed in Darnassian by the
+translation of <em>Zin-Azshari</em> as "Glory of Azshara." The Nazja phrase <em>Zin-Azshari adore</em>
+is a direct retention of this Darnassian formula — naga NPCs invoke the glory of their queen
+using the same morpheme. This is a clear case of Nazja preserving a Darnassian lexical item
+in a ritual/religious context.</p>
+
+<h3>Set 8: Blessing / benediction <span class="cog">*adore</span></h3>
+
+<table>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td class="reconstructed">*adore</td>
+  <td>Elune-adore</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>Zin-Azshari adore</td><td>be with you / bless</td>
+</tr>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> The morpheme <em>adore</em> appears in Darnassian as part of
+<em>Elune-adore</em> ("Elune be with you") and in Nazja as part of <em>Zin-Azshari adore</em>
+("Glory of Azshara be with you"). The structural parallel is exact: [deity/glory] + <em>adore</em>.
+In Nazja, Azshara has replaced Elune as the object of veneration, but the grammatical formula
+remains identical. This is strong evidence that <em>adore</em> is an inherited Proto-Elven
+benediction preserved in both languages.</p>
