+<h3>Investigation: Drust <em>Gol-</em> and Titan <em>Golganneth</em></h3>
+
+<div class="note">
+<strong>Question:</strong> Is the Drust <em>Gol-</em> place-name prefix related to the
+<em>Gol-</em> element in the Titan Pantheon name <em>Golganneth</em> (Lord of the Skies
+and Roaring Oceans)?
+
+<h4>Arguments for a connection:</h4>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>Phonological identity:</strong> The morpheme <em>Gol-</em> /ɡɔl/ is phonologically
+identical in both Drust and Titan. Short morphemes of this shape (CVC with back vowels) are
+common cross-linguistically, but the exact match in two related languages is noteworthy.</li>
+<li><strong>Genetic relationship:</strong> Drust is a grand-daughter of Titan (via Vrykul).
+A Titan morpheme <em>Gol-</em> could have been inherited by the Drust through their Vrykul
+ancestors, even if mainland Vrykul lost it.</li>
+<li><strong>Structural parallel:</strong> Both <em>Gol-</em> in Drust and <em>Uld-</em> in
+Titan serve as place-naming prefixes. If <em>Gol-</em> is a Titan morpheme, it may have
+functioned similarly to <em>Uld-</em> in the parent language, with Drust preserving
+<em>Gol-</em> while Vrykul (and Dwarven) lost both <em>Gol-</em> and <em>Uld-</em> in
+favor of suffix-based naming.</li>
+<li><strong>Thornspeaker preservation:</strong> The Drust Thornspeaker tradition maintained
+a connection to druidism and, through it, to titan-forged origins. The Thornspeakers may
+have preserved a Titan morpheme (<em>Gol-</em>) that mainland Vrykul lost when they
+adopted Norse-style naming conventions.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h4>Arguments against a connection:</h4>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>Semantic mismatch:</strong> If <em>Gol-</em> means "settlement/place/fortress"
+in Drust, it is unclear what it would mean in <em>Golganneth</em>. A Pantheon member's name
+containing "settlement" seems semantically unlikely. Possible resolutions:
+  <ul>
+    <li><em>Gol-</em> had a broader Titan meaning ("great/primary/mighty/dominant") that
+    narrowed in Drust to "stronghold/settlement" — i.e., "the great place" → "fortress"</li>
+    <li><em>Gol-</em> meant "stone" in Titan (cf. the stone construction of Drust
+    fortresses), and <em>Golganneth</em> = "Stone-[ganneth]" — though this is a poor
+    semantic fit for a sky/ocean deity</li>
+    <li><em>Gol-</em> in <em>Golganneth</em> and <em>Gol-</em> in Drust are unrelated
+    homophones</li>
+  </ul>
+</li>
+<li><strong>Absence in Vrykul:</strong> No <em>Gol-</em> morpheme is attested in mainland
+Vrykul place names or vocabulary. If <em>Gol-</em> were a genuine Titan morpheme inherited
+by Drust through Vrykul, some trace might be expected in Vrykul — unless Vrykul
+systematically replaced all Titan prefixes with suffixes, which is consistent with the
+observed replacement of <em>Uld-</em>.</li>
+<li><strong>Small sample size:</strong> The Drust corpus consists of only 4 <em>Gol-</em>
+place names, and the Titan <em>Gol-</em> appears only once (in <em>Golganneth</em>).
+Two data points are insufficient to establish a sound correspondence.</li>
+<li><strong>Celtic parallel:</strong> The real-world Irish/Celtic word <em>gall</em>
+("stone/rock/foreigner") may have inspired both Drust <em>Gol-</em> and Titan
+<em>Golganneth</em> independently, as Blizzard's writers drew from Celtic and Norse
+mythology simultaneously.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h4>Conclusion:</h4>
+<p>The phonological match between Drust <em>Gol-</em> and the <em>Gol-</em> element in
+Titan <em>Golganneth</em> is intriguing but remains <strong>speculative</strong>. The most
+plausible scenario, if the connection is real, is that <em>Gol-</em> was a Titan morpheme
+with a broad meaning like "great/mighty/primary" that narrowed semantically in Drust to
+"stronghold/fortress" (the great/mighty place), while in <em>Golganneth</em> it retained
+its original broader sense. The structural parallel with Titan <em>Uld-</em> (both being
+prefix-based place-naming) supports the hypothesis that Drust <em>Gol-</em> is a retention
+from an earlier stage of the titan-forged language family. However, coincidence cannot be
+ruled out, and the semantic gap between "settlement" and a Pantheon name element remains
+the strongest argument against the connection. <strong>Confidence: Low.</strong></p>
+</div>
+
