<h2 id="apostrophe">Apostrophe Patterns</h2>

<p>The most striking shared feature across all four languages is the use of apostrophes
to mark morpheme boundaries. However, the <em>structural position</em> and <em>function</em>
of the apostrophe differ significantly between Shath'Yar and the other three languages.</p>

<h3>Pattern 1: Initial Apostrophe (Shath'Yar dominant)</h3>

<p>In Shath'Yar, the apostrophe typically separates a <strong>single initial consonant</strong>
from the remainder of the name. This suggests the initial consonant is itself a morpheme
(possibly a prefix or determiner).</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Name</th><th>IPA</th><th>Decomposition</th><th>Initial Element</th><th>Root</th></tr>
<tr><td><em>N'Zoth</em></td><td class="ipa">/n&#712;zo&#650;&#952;/</td><td>N' + Zoth</td><td class="inferred">N' (negation/absence?)</td><td>Zoth</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>C'Thun</em></td><td class="ipa">/k&#601;&#712;&#952;u&#720;n/</td><td>C' + Thun</td><td class="inferred">C' (unknown)</td><td>Thun</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>N'raqi</em></td><td class="ipa">/n&#712;r&#593;&#720;ki/</td><td>N' + raqi</td><td class="inferred">N' (negation/absence?)</td><td>raq + -i</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>K'thir</em></td><td class="ipa">/k&#712;&#952;&#618;r/</td><td>K' + thir</td><td class="inferred">K' (servant/thrall?)</td><td>th + -ir</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>Y'Shaarj</em></td><td class="ipa">/j&#712;&#643;&#593;&#720;rd&#658;/</td><td>Y' + Shaarj</td><td class="inferred">Y' (unknown)</td><td>Shaarj</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>Il'gynoth</em></td><td class="ipa">/&#618;l&#712;&#609;&#618;n&#594;&#952;/</td><td>Il' + gynoth</td><td class="inferred">Il (heart/core?)</td><td>gynoth</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Pattern 2: Medial Apostrophe (nerubian, qiraji, ethereal dominant)</h3>

<p>In nerubian, qiraji, and ethereal, the apostrophe typically separates <strong>two content
morphemes</strong> &mdash; a true compound boundary, not a prefix-stem division.</p>

<table>
