<h2 id="transmission">Transmission Hypotheses</h2>

<p>The shared apostrophe convention across all four languages requires explanation.
Three broad hypotheses are evaluated:</p>

<h3>Hypothesis 1: Blizzard Design Aesthetic</h3>

<p>Apostrophe-marked names are a standard fantasy convention for "alien" or "ancient" names.
Blizzard applied this convention consistently to all non-titan-forged, non-elven species
without implying in-universe linguistic connections. <strong>Assessment:</strong>
<span class="confidence-high">High</span> plausibility as at least a contributing factor.</p>

<h3>Hypothesis 2: Old God &rarr; Aqir &rarr; Nerubian/Qiraji Transmission</h3>

<pre>
Shath'Yar (Old God language; initial apostrophe = prefix boundary)
    &darr; (Old Gods created the Aqir)
Aqir (inherited apostrophe convention; shifted to medial = compound boundary)
    &darr; (Aqir diverged)
    &boxv;&boxvr;&ndash; Nerubian (retained medial apostrophe compounding)
    &boxv;&boxvr;&ndash; Qiraji (retained medial apostrophe compounding)
    &boxur;&ndash; [Substrate in Nazja via naga/Old God contact]
</pre>

<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> <span class="confidence-mod">Medium&ndash;High</span> plausibility
for the Shath'Yar &rarr; Aqir &rarr; nerubian/qiraji chain. The ethereal language sits
outside this chain.</p>

<h3>Hypothesis 3: Convergent Development</h3>

<p>Multiple alien languages independently developed apostrophe-marked compounding because
their phonological systems all feature morpheme boundaries at points of phonotactic transition.
<strong>Assessment:</strong> <span class="confidence-mod">Medium</span> plausibility. Most
likely, the truth combines Hypothesis 1 (Blizzard aesthetic) and Hypothesis 2 (in-universe
transmission for Aqir-descended languages).</p>

<h3>Comprehensive Assessment</h3>

