content = r'''
<h2 id="grammar">Shared Grammatical Features</h2>

<p>Features confirmed across two or more of the four languages. Since no sentences are
attested in any language, only morphological and orthographic features can be compared.</p>

<h3>Confirmed Across All Four</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th><th>Shath'Yar</th><th>Nerubian</th><th>Qiraji</th><th>Ethereal</th></tr>
<tr><td>Apostrophe-marked boundary</td><td>Apostrophe separates morphemes in compounds</td><td>Yes (N'Zoth, C'Thun, Il'gynoth)</td><td>Yes (Anub'azal, Ovi'nax, Ahn'kahet)</td><td>Yes (Ahn'qiraj, Hive'Ashi)</td><td>Yes (K'aresh, Ve'nari)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Right-headed compounding</td><td>Head element is the second (right) element</td><td>Yes (N'+Zoth, C'+Thun)</td><td>Yes (Anub+azal, Azj+Kahet)</td><td>Yes (Ahn+qiraj)</td><td>Yes (K'+aresh, Tazav+esh)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Shared by Nerubian and Qiraji (Aqir Substrate)</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th><th>Evidence</th></tr>
<tr><td>Medial apostrophe compound boundary</td><td>Apostrophe separates two content morphemes</td><td>Nerubian: *Anub'azal*; Qiraji: *Ahn'qiraj*</td></tr>
<tr class="aqir"><td>*Ahn-* prefix</td><td>Shared Aqir morpheme meaning "old/ancient/sacred"</td><td>Nerubian: *Ahn'kahet*; Qiraji: *Ahn'qiraj*</td></tr>
<tr><td>Self-designation in toponym</td><td>The people's name appears in their major place names</td><td>Nerubian: *Azjol-Nerub*; Qiraji: *Ahn'qiraj*</td></tr>
<tr><td>Egyptian-inspired naming</td><td>Both use Egyptian aesthetic in proper names</td><td>Nerubian: *Anub-*, *Neferess*; Qiraji: *Ossirian*, *Anubisath*</td></tr>
<tr><td>Geminate simplification (nerubian)</td><td>Qiraji retains Aqir geminates; nerubian simplifies</td><td>Qiraji: *-naxx*; Nerubian: *-nax*</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Unique to Individual Languages</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Language</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>Initial apostrophe (C'+root)</td><td>Shath'Yar</td><td>Apostrophe marks prefix boundary, not compound boundary. The initial single consonant is itself a morpheme.</td></tr>
<tr><td>*-kahet* place suffix</td><td>Nerubian</td><td>Productive place-name suffix meaning "kingdom/realm"; 2 attestations spanning 16 years</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extreme apostrophe consistency</td><td>Nerubian</td><td>Nearly all multi-morpheme names use apostrophes; more consistent than any other WoW language</td></tr>
<tr><td>High /ks/ (x) frequency</td><td>Nerubian</td><td>6+ names with /ks/ or /x/; diagnostic of nerubian phonology</td></tr>
<tr><td>Geminate consonants in suffixes</td><td>Qiraji</td><td>*-naxx*, *-axx* (nerubian has simplified *-nax*, *-ax*)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Egyptian-inspired aesthetic</td><td>Qiraji (also Tol'vir)</td><td>Names like *Ossirian*, *Anubisath* evoke Egyptian phonology</td></tr>
<tr><td>*-esh* / *-aresh* locative suffix</td><td>Ethereal</td><td>Place-name suffix meaning "world/realm"; may have full root form *resh-*</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nexus-* title hierarchy</td><td>Ethereal</td><td>Common-language title system (Nexus-King, Nexus-Prince)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broker&ndash;Ethereal dialect variation</td><td>Ethereal</td><td>Shorter names in broker dialect; shared *-esh* suffix; same language with dialectal split</td></tr>
</table>

<hr>

<h3>Summary of Key Findings</h3>

<ol>
<li><strong>Two distinct apostrophe patterns:</strong> Shath'Yar uses initial apostrophes
    (prefix boundaries: C'+root), while nerubian, qiraji, and ethereal use medial apostrophes
    (compound boundaries: stem+'stem). This structural difference suggests independent
    development or a functional shift during the Old God &rarr; Aqir transmission.</li>

<li><strong>Aqir substrate morphemes emerging:</strong> At least two morphemes (*Ahn-* =
    "old/sacred" and *Azj-* = "deep/realm") appear to be shared Aqir heritage between
    nerubian and qiraji/naga. A third (*-naxx*/*-nax*) is possible but very low confidence.</li>

<li><strong>Potential first regular sound correspondence:</strong> If the geminate
    simplification hypothesis is correct (Aqir *-naxx* &rarr; nerubian *-nax*), this would
    be the first established sound correspondence between Aqir-descended languages.</li>

<li><strong>Ethereal is independent:</strong> Despite sharing the apostrophe convention,
    the ethereal language shows no confirmed morpheme sharing with the Aqir-descended
    languages. Its apostrophe usage and soft phonological profile suggest either independent
    development or Blizzard aesthetic choice.</li>

<li><strong>Blizzard design aesthetic is a major factor:</strong> The apostrophe convention
    is a standard fantasy trope that Blizzard applied consistently to "alien" species.
    Any in-universe explanation must account for the fact that the convention extends to
    the ethereal language, which has no Azeroth origin.</li>
</ol>

<hr>
<p><em>Last updated: 2026-06-08. All morpheme decompositions and reconstructed forms are
provisional based on the limited attested corpus. No forms should be cited as established
fact. Full source data: <a href="shath-yar/vocabulary.md">shath-yar/vocabulary.md</a>,
<a href="nerubian/vocabulary.md">nerubian/vocabulary.md</a>,
<a href="qiraji/vocabulary.md">qiraji/vocabulary.md</a>,
<a href="ethereal/vocabulary.md">ethereal/vocabulary.md</a>.</em></p>

</body>
</html>
'''

