 - **Nazja** (Naga) is derived from Darnassian but has diverged significantly due to 10,000 years of underwater isolation and physiological transformation
 - **Tol'vir** is likely derived from Titan, sharing this ancestry with **Mogu** — both are titan-forged languages from stone construct races; Tol'vir shows strong Egyptian-inspired naming conventions
 - **Ethereal / Broker** is spoken by ethereals and brokers from the shattered world of K'aresh; it has no confirmed genetic relationship to any other WoW language family. The potential *-esh* locative suffix appears in both *K'aresh* (homeworld) and *Tazavesh* (broker capital). Ethereals and brokers likely speak dialects of the same language, with broker names tending to be shorter. Despite sharing the apostrophe convention with Shath'Yar, nerubian, and qiraji, this is likely due to Blizzard design aesthetics rather than in-universe contact (see [alien-comparative.html](alien-comparative.html))
 - **Murloc/Jinyu/Ankoan/Kobyss** form a language family entirely separate from both the titan-forged and Aqir-descended families. Murlocs speak **Nerglish** (gurgling phonology, syllabic consonants); **Jinyu** evolved from murlocs with dramatically increased phonological complexity (full vowel inventory, CV/CVC syllable structure); **Ankoan** descend from jinyu with moderate simplification; **Kobyss** are ankoan-descended but show near-total language loss, retaining only 3–5 Common words ("Animate bones", "Devour", "Violence") — the only known case of linguistic regression in WoW (see [earthen/vocabulary.html](earthen/vocabulary.html#kobyss))
-- **Hara'ni** is spoken by the Haranir, an ancient race described as "not really elves, not really trolls" — they have bat-like ears, small tusks, grayish-purple skin, and can shapeshift into bats. The language has no confirmed genetic relationship to any other WoW language family. The *Hara-* root appears in five attestations (ethnonym *Haranir*, language name *Hara'ni*, capital *Harandar*, faction *Hara'ti*, concept *Aln'hara*). The *-dar* toponymic suffix in *Harandar* may be borrowed from Earthen/Arathi (Khaz Algar areal feature). Three female names end in *-ia* / *-eia* / *-iea* (possibly phonologically conditioned variants of a feminine suffix). The *Greenspeaker* title (held by Na'layro) parallels the Drust *Thornspeaker* title but is most likely independent parallel development. Haranir names show very high apostrophe frequency (medial compound boundaries), comparable to nerubian. Geographic proximity to Azj-Kahet makes nerubian loanwords plausible but none are confirmed (see [haranir/](haranir/))
+- **Hara'ni** is spoken by the Haranir, an ancient race described as "not really elves, not really trolls" — they have bat-like ears, small tusks, grayish-purple skin, and can shapeshift into bats. The language has no confirmed genetic relationship to any other WoW language family. The *Hara-* root appears in five attestations (ethnonym *Haranir*, language name *Hara'ni*, capital *Harandar*, faction *Hara'ti*, concept *Aln'hara*). The *Aln-* root now has two attestations (*Aln'hara* "sacred call" and *Alndust* "sacred dust" from Midnight). The *-dar* toponymic suffix in *Harandar* may be borrowed from Earthen/Arathi (Khaz Algar areal feature). Three female names end in *-ia* / *-eia* / *-iea* (possibly phonologically conditioned variants of a feminine suffix). The *Greenspeaker* title (held by Na'layro) parallels the Drust *Thornspeaker* title but is most likely independent parallel development. Haranir names show very high apostrophe frequency (medial compound boundaries), comparable to nerubian. Geographic proximity to Azj-Kahet makes nerubian loanwords plausible but none are confirmed. The Midnight expansion introduced the **ruutani** (a Harandar-native race; name may be Hara'ni) and the **Alndust** material (confirming the *Aln-* root), and revealed the Haranir have an unnamed goddess (see [haranir/](haranir/))

