 apostrophe-marked compounds outside the five core languages: <em>Hash'ey</em> (Zandali title
 meaning "unifier") and <em>Mor'duun</em> (leader of Twilight's Blade). Both follow the medial
 apostrophe compound pattern, reinforcing that Blizzard treats the apostrophe as a general
 alien/exotic marker across multiple language families.
+<br><br>
+<strong>Naaru names share the Shath'Yar initial-apostrophe pattern:</strong>
+All six attested Naaru names (A'dal, K'ure, K'ara, M'uru, O'ros, L'ura) follow the
+C'V(C) structure identical to Shath'Yar names (N'Zoth, C'Thun, Y'Shaarj). This is
+paradoxical because Naaru are beings of Holy Light &mdash; the antithesis of Old Gods and
+the Void. Three interpretations: (1) the naming convention predates the Light-Void split;
+(2) the apostrophe pattern is a universal cosmic naming convention; (3) Blizzard design
+convergence applied to all "otherworldly" names. See
+<a href="shath-yar/vocabulary.html#naaru">shath-yar/vocabulary.html &sect; Naaru Names</a>
+for full analysis.
