+<!-- ==================== THE -OSH SUFFIX ==================== -->
+
+<h3 id="osh-suffix">The <em>-osh</em> Suffix in Names</h3>
+
+<p>Three prominent orcish personal names share a final <em>-osh</em> element:</p>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr><th>Name</th><th>Attested Context</th><th>Known Meaning</th></tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td>Grommash</td><td>Son of Grom Hellscream, Warchief of the True Horde</td><td>No confirmed translation</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Garrosh</td><td>Son of Grom Hellscream, Warchief of the Horde</td><td>No confirmed translation</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Lok'osh</td><td>Spirit Walker title/term</td><td>No confirmed translation</td></tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<h4>Analysis 1: <em>-osh</em> as a name-forming suffix meaning &ldquo;son of&rdquo; or &ldquo;scion of&rdquo;</h4>
+
+<p>Both <em>Grommash</em> and <em>Garrosh</em> are sons of prominent orcish figures (Grommash = Grom's son; Garrosh = also Grom's son, though the &ldquo;Gar-&rdquo; element is unexplained). If <em>-osh</em> = &ldquo;son/scion of,&rdquo; then:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><em>Grommash</em> = <em>Gromm</em> + <em>-ash</em> (not <em>-osh</em>) &mdash; contradicts this analysis unless <em>-ash</em> and <em>-osh</em> are allomorphs</li>
+<li><em>Garrosh</em> = <em>Garr</em> + <em>-osh</em> &mdash; plausible if <em>Garr-</em> is a personal name element</li>
+<li><em>Lok'osh</em> = <em>Lok'</em> + <em>-osh</em> &mdash; &ldquo;Scion of Victory&rdquo;? Plausible semantically</li>
+</ul>
+<p>The problem is that <em>Grommash</em> ends in <em>-ash</em>, not <em>-osh</em>. If <em>-ash</em> and <em>-osh</em> are allomorphs, the conditioning environment is unclear (both follow <span class="ipa">/r/</span> in a doubled consonant: <em>Grommash</em> <span class="ipa">/m:/</span>, <em>Garrosh</em> <span class="ipa">/r:/</span>).</p>
+
+<h4>Analysis 2: <em>-osh</em> as a valor/warrior suffix</h4>
+
+<p>If <em>Lok'</em> = &ldquo;victory&rdquo; (as inferred from <em>Lok'tar</em>), then <em>Lok'osh</em> could mean &ldquo;victorious warrior&rdquo; or &ldquo;one of victory.&rdquo; Extending this:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><em>-osh</em> could be a suffix meaning &ldquo;warrior,&rdquo; &ldquo;champion,&rdquo; or &ldquo;one who embodies [root]&rdquo;</li>
+<li><em>Garrosh</em> = <em>Garr-</em> + <em>-osh</em> = &ldquo;warrior of [Garr-]&rdquo; &mdash; the <em>Garr-</em> root is unknown</li>
+<li><em>Grommash</em> would need <em>-ash</em> as a variant, which weakens the analysis</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h4>Analysis 3: <em>-osh</em> as a coincidental phonological pattern</h4>
+
+<p>The simplest explanation: <em>-osh</em> is not a morpheme at all. The three names share a final <span class="ipa">/ʃ/</span> sound, but:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><em>Grommash</em> ends in <span class="ipa">/aʃ/</span>, <em>Garrosh</em> in <span class="ipa">/oʃ/</span>, <em>Lok'osh</em> in <span class="ipa">/oʃ/</span> &mdash; the vowel quality differs</li>
+<li><span class="ipa">/ʃ/</span> is a common coda in orcish names (<em>Grommash</em>, <em>Garrosh</em>, <em>Kosh'harg</em> &rarr; <em>harg</em> ends in <span class="ipa">/g/</span>, but the <span class="ipa">/ʃ/</span> coda is still notable)</li>
+<li>Proper names in many languages share phonological patterns without shared morphology (cf. English <em>Richard</em>, <em>Edward</em>, <em>Howard</em> all ending in <span class="ipa">/d/</span> &mdash; this does not make <span class="ipa">/-d/</span> a suffix)</li>
+</ul>
+
+<div class="note">
+<strong>Confidence: Very Low&ndash;Low</strong> for <em>-osh</em> as a genuine morphological suffix. The strongest evidence is <em>Lok'osh</em>, where <em>Lok'</em> is a known root (&ldquo;victory&rdquo;) and <em>-osh</em> would complete the compound meaningfully. However, the <em>Grommash</em> / <em>Garrosh</em> data is ambiguous: <em>Grommash</em> ends in <em>-ash</em> (not <em>-osh</em>), and no meaning for <em>Garr-</em> is known. The possibility that <em>-osh</em> is a Blizzard aesthetic pattern (a &ldquo;guttural-sounding&rdquo; ending applied to warrior names) rather than a genuine morpheme cannot be excluded.
+</div>
+
