+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td>Stops</td><td></td><td></td><td>t, d</td><td></td><td>k, g</td><td></td></tr>
+<tr><td>Fricatives</td><td>f, v</td><td><span class="ipa">θ</span> (th)</td><td>s, z</td><td><span class="ipa">ʃ</span> (sh)</td><td></td><td>h</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Nasals</td><td></td><td></td><td>m, n</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
+<tr><td>Liquids</td><td></td><td></td><td>l, r</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p>Notes:</p>
 <ul>
-<li>Shalassian evolved from Darnassian over 10,000 years of isolation under the Nightwell's shield.</li>
-<li>The phonology likely shares features with Darnassian and Thalassian: fluid consonants, vowel-heavy syllables, and melodic intonation <span class="inferred">(inferred from elven language family patterns)</span>.</li>
-<li>Apostrophes mark morpheme boundaries, consistent with other elven languages (confirmed by Shal'dorei, Shal'Aran).</li>
+<li><span class="ipa">/k/</span> and <span class="ipa">/g/</span> appear only in Nightborne proper names (<em>Oculeth</em>, <em>Silgryn</em>); their status as regular phonemes is uncertain. They may be inherited from the broader Highborne dialect of Darnassian, in which they were marginal.</li>
+<li><span class="ipa">/v/</span> appears in <em>Val'sharah</em>, but this name may be Darnassian rather than Shalassian.</li>
+<li><span class="ipa">/f/</span> is not directly attested in Shalassian names but is inferred from its presence in Darnassian (<em>falah</em>) and the broader elven consonant inventory.</li>
+<li><span class="ipa">/z/</span> is not directly attested in Shalassian proper but is inferred from the broader elven inventory; the absence may be a corpus gap rather than a genuine phonological difference.</li>
+<li><span class="ipa">/θ/</span> is retained from Darnassian, appearing in <em>Thalyssra</em>, <em>Ly'leth</em>, <em>Oculeth</em>. Its consistent presence confirms Shalassian's membership in the elven language family.</li>
+<li>No affricates (<span class="ipa">/tʃ/</span>, <span class="ipa">/dʒ/</span>) are attested in Shalassian, unlike Nazja. This is consistent with the language's conservative phonological development under isolation.</li>
 </ul>
 
+<h3 id="vowel-inventory">Vowel Inventory</h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr><th></th><th>Front</th><th>Central</th><th>Back</th></tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td>Close</td><td>i</td><td></td><td>u</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Mid</td><td>e</td><td></td><td>o</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Open</td><td></td><td>a</td><td></td></tr>
+</tbody>
