+<li><strong>Dental fricative <span class="ipa">/θ/</span></strong>: Retained from Darnassian. Appears in <em>Thalyssra</em>, <em>Ly'leth</em>, <em>Oculeth</em>. This is a key diagnostic feature distinguishing Shalassian from Nazja (which lost <span class="ipa">/θ/</span>) and confirming its closer relationship to Darnassian and Thalassian.</li>
+
+<li><strong>Liquid frequency</strong>: <span class="ipa">/l/</span> and <span class="ipa">/r/</span> are very common, as in all elven languages: <em>Thalyssra</em>, <em>Silgryn</em>, <em>Lunastre</em>, <em>Shal'dorei</em>. This produces the characteristic melodic quality shared across the elven family.</li>
+
+<li><strong>Sibilant profile</strong>: <span class="ipa">/ʃ/</span> is common (<em>Shal'dorei</em>, <em>Shal'Aran</em>), consistent with Darnassian. Unlike Nazja, Shalassian does not show <span class="ipa">/ʒ/</span> or increased sibilant density, confirming that these are Nazja-specific innovations.</li>
+
+<li><strong>Sonorant-heavy word endings</strong>: As in Darnassian and Thalassian, Shalassian names prefer sonorant endings: <em>-ei</em> (dorei), <em>-an</em> (Aran), <em>-ra</em> (Thalyssra). This is a stable elven family feature.</li>
+
+<li><strong>Apostrophe as morpheme boundary (not phonological)</strong>: Apostrophes in Shalassian mark morpheme boundaries (<em>Shal'dorei</em>, <em>Shal'Aran</em>, <em>Ly'leth</em>, <em>Tel'anor'ien</em>), not glottal stops or phonological processes. This is consistent with the elven family convention.</li>
+
+<li><strong>Conservative phonology</strong>: Shalassian appears phonologically conservative relative to Darnassian, retaining the core consonant and vowel inventory with minimal innovation. This is consistent with the Nightborne's isolation &mdash; without contact with other languages, the phonological pressure for change would be reduced compared to Thalassian (which had extensive contact with Troll and Common) or Nazja (which underwent physiological transformation).</li>
+</ol>
+
