 <!-- ============================================================ -->
+<h2 id="history">History</h2>
+
+<p>Darnassian is among the oldest living languages on Azeroth, with an unbroken tradition stretching back over ten thousand years to the era of the Well of Eternity.</p>
+
+<p><strong>Pre-Sundering (&minus;10,000 years)</strong>: Darnassian was the common tongue of the Kaldorei Empire, spoken from the Zin-Azshari capital to the farthest reaches of night elf civilization. The Highborne (<em>Quel'dorei</em>) spoke a more formal register associated with the court of Queen Azshara, while the common Kaldorei used a vernacular form. During this period, the language produced the naming conventions still visible today: the <em>-dorei</em> ethnonym suffix, the <em>-drassil</em> World Tree naming pattern, and the honorific compounds <em>Shan'do</em> / <em>Thero'shan</em>.</p>
+
+<p><strong>The War of the Ancients and the Great Sundering</strong>: The catastrophic Sundering shattered the Kaldorei Empire. The Highborne who followed Azshara into the ocean depths were transformed into the naga, and their language diverged into Nazja. The Highborne who remained on land but continued practicing arcane magic were exiled and eventually developed Thalassian. The Kaldorei who survived renounced arcane magic and adopted druidism, and Darnassian continued as their tongue.</p>
+
+<p><strong>The Long Vigil (&minus;10,000 to &minus;3 years)</strong>: For nearly ten millennia, the night elves of Mount Hyjal maintained Darnassian in relative isolation. During this period, the druidic tradition shaped the language&rsquo;s vocabulary: terms for natural phenomena, the Emerald Dream, and shapeshifting were encoded in Darnassian. The Shan'do / Thero'shan master-apprentice system preserved linguistic stability through oral transmission.</p>
+
+<p><strong>Third War and Aftermath (Warcraft III &ndash; Vanilla WoW)</strong>: The return of the Burning Legion forced the night elves out of isolation. Darnassian speakers encountered Common, Thalassian, and Orcish for the first time in millennia. The planting of Teldrassil and founding of Darnassus created a new cultural center, though Nordrassil on Mount Hyjal remained sacred.</p>
+
+<p><strong>Destruction of Teldrassil (Battle for Azeroth)</strong>: The burning of Teldrassil by Sylvanas Windrunner was a catastrophic event for the Kaldorei people. The physical heart of modern Darnassian culture was destroyed, but the language survived through its dispersed speakers. Darnassian continues to be spoken in night elf settlements across Azeroth.</p>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<h2 id="dialectal-variation">Dialectal Variation</h2>
+
+<h3>Highborne Register</h3>
+
+<p>The Highborne (<em>Quel'dorei</em>) who survived the Sundering without being transformed retained a more formal, archaic register of Darnassian. This register is characterized by:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Retention of older morphological patterns</strong>: The Highborne maintained the full <em>-dorei</em> ethnonym suffix and the <em>Quel'</em> (&ldquo;noble/high&rdquo;) prefix as living morphemes, whereas common Darnassian may have partially lexicalized these into proper names.</li>
+  <li><strong>Arcane vocabulary</strong>: The Highborne register presumably contained terminology for arcane magic (<em>Elunara</em> &mdash; &ldquo;Grace of Elune,&rdquo; though this may be shared), ritual incantation, and the philosophical framework of arcane practice. Much of this vocabulary is lost or unattested.</li>
+  <li><strong>Formality and prosody</strong>: The Highborne register likely maintained a more measured, formal prosody compared to the vernacular Kaldorei speech. The limited attested Highborne-associated terms (<em>Zin-Azshari</em>, <em>Ban'dinoriel</em>) suggest polysyllabic, honorific-heavy constructions.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>The surviving Highborne in Dire Maul and elsewhere may preserve elements of this register, but no Highborne-specific phrases are attested in-game with translations.</p>
+
+<h3>Modern Darnassian vs. Ancient Forms</h3>
+
+<p>The Darnassian spoken in the current era is essentially the same language as that spoken before the Sundering, owing to the night elves&rsquo; extreme longevity and strong oral tradition. However, some differences are plausible:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Druidic neologisms</strong>: Terms related to the Emerald Dream, druidic shapeshifting, and Cenarius&rsquo;s teachings likely entered the language after the Sundering, as druidism was adopted as the Kaldorei&rsquo;s new path.</li>
+  <li><strong>Loss of arcane terminology</strong>: After the ban on arcane magic, vocabulary related to spellcasting and the Well of Eternity may have fallen into disuse or acquired negative connotations.</li>
+  <li><strong>Place name fossilization</strong>: Many pre-Sundering place names (<em>Zin-Azshari</em>, <em>Suramar</em>) survive as proper names whose etymologies may no longer be transparent to speakers.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>Elven Comparative Perspective</h3>
+
+<p>Darnassian&rsquo;s daughter languages show how the same root language diverged under radically different circumstances:</p>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th></tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td>Divergence date</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>~&minus;7,000 years</td><td>~&minus;10,000 years</td><td>~&minus;10,000 years</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Environment</td><td>Forest/mountain</td><td>Temperate (Quel'Thalas)</td><td>Sealed city (Suramar)</td><td>Underwater</td></tr>
+<tr><td><span class="ipa">/ʒ/</span> (zh)</td><td>Absent</td><td>Absent</td><td>Absent</td><td>Very frequent</td></tr>
+<tr><td><span class="ipa">/dʒ/</span> (j)</td><td>Absent</td><td>Absent</td><td>Present</td><td>Very frequent</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>-dorei</em> suffix</td><td>Present</td><td>Present</td><td>Present</td><td>Present</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Apostrophe usage</td><td>Morpheme boundary</td><td>Morpheme boundary</td><td>Morpheme boundary</td><td>Morpheme boundary</td></tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
