+<h3 id="ishnu-greeting-root">The <em>Ishnu</em> Greeting Root</h3>
+
+<p>Two additional canon phrases share the root <em>Ishnu</em>, expanding the Darnassian greeting/blessing corpus:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>Ishnu-alah</strong> &mdash; &ldquo;Good fortune / blessings upon you&rdquo; (Warcraft III Priestess of the Moon voice line)</li>
+<li><strong>Ishnu-dal-die</strong> &mdash; &ldquo;Good fortune to you / farewell variant&rdquo; (Warcraft III voice line)</li>
+</ul>
+
+<p><em>Ishnu</em> appears to be a greeting or blessing particle meaning &ldquo;good fortune&rdquo; or &ldquo;blessings.&rdquo; The element <em>-alah</em> may connect to Thalassian <em>alah</em> (&ldquo;light&rdquo;), suggesting either a shared elven root or a cross-language borrowing. The element <em>-dal-die</em> is opaque; <em>dal</em> may relate to Thalassian <em>Dal</em> (possibly &ldquo;fortune&rdquo;) and <em>die</em> may be a dative or second-person marker, but this is <span class="uncertain">[speculative]</span>.</p>
+
+<p>Two further expanded-source phrases are attested but with uncertain reliability:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>Elune-melor</strong> &mdash; &ldquo;Elune grant strength&rdquo; or similar; follows the <em>Elune-</em> + hyphen + complement pattern established by <em>Elune-adore</em></li>
+<li><strong>Torel na finel</strong> &mdash; A phrase of unknown meaning; <em>na</em> may be a preposition or particle</li>
+</ul>
+
