-<h3>Phonological Observations</h3>
+<h3>Linguistic Contact Analysis</h3>
 
 <ul>
-  <li><strong>Tone:</strong> Romanized Pandaren names suggest a tonal language, consistent with Chinese cultural inspiration. Tone cannot be confirmed from romanized text alone.</li>
-  <li><strong>Syllable structure:</strong> Predominantly (C)V(N) — simple open or lightly closed syllables (Shen, Jin, Xuen, Su).</li>
-  <li><strong>Apostrophe usage:</strong> Yu'lon contains an apostrophe, possibly marking a morpheme boundary or glottal stop.</li>
-  <li><strong>Hyphen usage:</strong> Shen-zin Su uses hyphens, possibly marking phrase boundaries.</li>
-  <li><strong>Name reduplication:</strong> <em>Li Li</em> demonstrates reduplication, consistent with East Asian language patterns.</li>
+  <li><strong>Substrate influence:</strong> The Mogu dominated Pandaren culture for millennia. Pandaren place names in the Vale of Eternal Blossoms and surrounding areas may preserve Mogu toponyms. <em>Mogu'shan</em> is the clearest example: the element <em>-shan</em> likely originates from Mogu, not Pandaren.</li>
+  <li><strong>Loan direction:</strong> Given the power dynamic, most loanwords likely flowed from Mogu (dominant language) into Pandaren (substrate language). This is consistent with real-world patterns of linguistic borrowing under domination.</li>
+  <li><strong>Sha vocabulary:</strong> The word <em>Sha</em> for the negative emotion entities in Mists of Pandaria may be related to Shath'Yar (Old God language) rather than either Pandaren or Mogu. The Sha are remnants of Y'Shaarj's power, which predates both races.</li>
+  <li><strong>Titan vocabulary:</strong> Both Pandaren and Mogu share exposure to Titan facilities in Pandaria (Mogu'shan Vaults, Terrace of Endless Spring). Some place names may have Titan-language origins that predate both peoples.</li>
+  <li><strong>Zandali influence:</strong> The Zandalari trolls invaded Pandaria and had extensive contact with both Mogu and Pandaren. The element <em>-jin</em> may be a Zandali borrowing present in both languages.</li>
 </ul>
 
+<p><strong>Methodological caveat:</strong> Both Pandaren and Mogu have extremely limited attestation. Cross-referencing two poorly attested languages multiplies uncertainty. The connections above are hypotheses, not confirmed relationships.</p>
+
 </body>
 </html>

