+<h3>Kith'ix: C'Thraxxi General and the Founding of Azj'Aqir</h3>
+
+<p><em>Kith'ix</em> is a <a href="https://warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/Kith%27ix">C'Thrax</a>
+(a type of N'raqi / faceless one) who served the Old Gods during the Black Empire.
+After the Black Empire fell, Kith'ix was buried at the base of the Zandalar Mountains.
+Reawakened by the sentient dagger <em>Xal'atath</em>, it rallied the Aqir into founding
+the empire of <em>Azj'Aqir</em> and waged the <em>Aqir and Troll War</em>. The Amani tribe
+ultimately killed Kith'ix, and the city of <em>Zul'Aman</em> was built atop its resting place.</p>
+
+<p>Kith'ix is significant for the Aqir substrate analysis because:</p>
+
+<ol>
+<li><strong>It founded <em>Azj'Aqir</em></strong> &mdash; the Aqir empire whose name contains
+the <span class="inferred">*Azj-*</span> morpheme analyzed above. Kith'ix is the direct link
+between the Shath'Yar-speaking Old God forces and the Aqir, providing an in-universe
+transmission vector for the apostrophe-marked naming convention.</li>
+
+<li><strong>Its name follows the medial apostrophe pattern</strong>: <em>Kith'ix</em>
+(<span class="inferred">*Kith'*</span> + <span class="inferred">*-ix*</span>), consistent
+with Aqir-descended compound naming rather than the Shath'Yar initial-apostrophe pattern.
+If *-ix* is a genuine suffix, it may relate to the nerubian name <em>Ix'zek</em>
+(which has *Ix-* as an initial element). The *-ix* / *Ix-* alternation could reflect
+Aqir morpheme ordering flexibility or simply coincidental phonological similarity.</li>
+
+<li><strong>It used Xal'atath as a weapon</strong> &mdash; linking Kith'ix directly to
+the Shath'Yar language community. The dagger <em>Xal'atath</em> itself follows the
+Shath'Yar initial-apostrophe pattern (*Xal'* + *-atath*), but Kith'ix's own name
+uses the medial pattern, suggesting the Aqir shifted from prefix-marking to
+compound-marking after transmission from the Old Gods.</li>
+
+<li><strong>Atal'Utek was built to commemorate the victory over Kith'ix</strong> &mdash;
+connecting Zandali naming (*Atal* = "devoted") to the Aqir/Troll War. This is the only
+known case where a Zandali place name directly references a Shath'Yar-origin being.</li>
+</ol>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr><th>Name</th><th>Language Family</th><th>Apostrophe Position</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td><em>Kith'ix</em></td><td>Shath'Yar (C'Thraxxi)</td><td>Medial (compound boundary)</td><td>C'Thrax who founded Azj'Aqir; killed by Amani; Zul'Aman built on its grave</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>Xal'atath</em></td><td>Shath'Yar</td><td>Medial (compound boundary)</td><td>Sentient dagger used by Kith'ix; also used by players in Legion</td></tr>
+<tr><td><em>Azj'Aqir</em></td><td>Aqir</td><td>Medial (compound boundary)</td><td>Empire founded by Kith'ix; contains *Azj-* morpheme</td></tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<div class="note">
+<strong>Transmission implication:</strong> Kith'ix provides the missing link in the
+Shath'Yar &rarr; Aqir transmission chain. As a C'Thraxxi general who directly commanded
+the Aqir, Kith'ix would have been the vector through which Old God naming conventions
+(including apostrophe-marked compounding) were transmitted to the Aqir language.
+The shift from Shath'Yar initial apostrophes (prefix boundaries) to Aqir medial apostrophes
+(compound boundaries) may reflect Kith'ix's own naming convention &mdash; <em>Kith'ix</em>
+already uses the medial pattern, not the initial one. This suggests the C'Thraxxi may have
+developed their own naming conventions distinct from the pure Shath'Yar pattern before
+transmitting them to the Aqir.
+<br><br>
+<strong>Confidence: Medium&ndash;High</strong> for Kith'ix as the historical vector of
+Shath'Yar &rarr; Aqir language transmission. The in-universe lore provides a clear
+historical narrative: Kith'ix (C'Thraxxi, Shath'Yar-speaking) &rarr; commands Aqir &rarr;
+Aqir inherit apostrophe convention.
+</div>
+
