+<h2 id="relationships">Relationship to Other Languages</h2>
+
+<h3>Vrykul (Direct Ancestor)</h3>
+
+<p>Common descends directly from Vrykul. Humans are the offspring of vrykul who were affected by the Curse of Flesh and grew smaller. The linguistic relationship is confirmed by:</p>
+<ul>
+<li>The wiki notes that Common shares many similar words with Vrykul, Dwarven, and Gnomish</li>
+<li>The place name <em>Tirisfal</em> is confirmed as a Vrykul word adopted into Common</li>
+<li>The suffix <span class="vrykul"><em>-garde</em></span> in <em>Stromgarde</em> is directly inherited from Vrykul <span class="vrykul"><em>-garde</em></span> (enclosure)</li>
+<li>Historical human names (<em>Thoradin</em>, <em>Lothar</em>) may contain Vrykul morpheme elements</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>Titan (Grandparent Language)</h3>
+
+<p>Common is a grand-daughter of Titan via Vrykul. Any Titan morphemes in Common would have been inherited through Vrykul. The evidence for direct Titan&rarr;Common borrowing is limited to what Vrykul preserved:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><em>Tirisfal</em> (Tyr's Fall) contains <span class="vrykul"><em>Tiris-</em></span> (Tyr), which traces to Titan <em>Tyr</em> (the Keeper)</li>
+<li><span class="vrykul"><em>-garde</em></span> traces to Vrykul <span class="vrykul"><em>-garde</em></span>, which replaced Titan <em>Uld-</em> naming</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>Dwarven and Gnomish (Parallel Descendants)</h3>
+
+<p>Common, Dwarven, and Gnomish all descend from Titan through different titan-forged lineages (Vrykul, Earthen, Mechagnome). They are parallel descendants, not ancestor-descendant. Shared features include:</p>
+<ul>
+<li>Right-headed compounding (inherited from Titan)</li>
+<li>Some shared vocabulary for basic concepts</li>
+<li>Similar phonological inventories (simplified from Titan)</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>Orcish (Contact Language)</h3>
+
+<p>Common and Orcish have been in contact since the First War. Extensive borrowing likely occurred in both directions, though no specific Common&rarr;Orcish or Orcish&rarr;Common loanwords have been documented in lore.</p>
+
+<h2 id="phonology">Key Phonological Features</h2>
+
+<ul>
+<li><strong>Simplified consonant clusters</strong>: Compared to Vrykul, Common likely has fewer complex initial clusters. Names like <em>Thoradin</em> and <em>Arthas</em> show simpler onsets than Vrykul names like <em>Vrykul</em> or <em>Skarvald</em>.</li>
+<li><strong>Loss of /y/</strong>: The Vrykul front rounded vowel /y/ (as in <em>Ymiron</em>, <em>Hyldnir</em>) would likely have been unrounded to /i/ in Common, as English-type languages do not maintain front rounded vowels.</li>
+<li><strong>Analytic syntax</strong>: Common likely developed more analytic syntax (relying on word order rather than inflection) as part of the simplification from Vrykul.</li>
+<li><strong>English as proxy</strong>: In-game, Common is rendered as English. Whether this means Common IS English-like (analytic, SVO, low inflection) or is simply translated into English for gameplay is unclear. The structural simplification from Vrykul is consistent with Common being English-like in typology.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="attestation">Attestation and Reliability</h2>
+
+<p>Common is a <strong>paradoxically attested</strong> language. It is the most frequently heard language in WoW (rendered as English), but as a linguistic object within the Warcraft universe, it has almost no distinct attestation. Everything said in &ldquo;Common&rdquo; is simply English, and no Blizzard publication has treated Common as a distinct constructed language with its own grammar or vocabulary.</p>
+
+<h3>What is reliable</h3>
+<ul>
+<li><em>Tirisfal</em> = &ldquo;Tyr's Fall&rdquo; (confirmed by <em>Chronicle Volume 1</em>).</li>
+<li>Humans descend from vrykul (confirmed by <em>Chronicle Volume 1</em>).</li>
+<li>Common shares many similar words with Vrykul, Dwarven, and Gnomish (noted by the wiki).</li>
+<li><span class="vrykul"><em>-garde</em></span> in <em>Stromgarde</em> is a Vrykul suffix (structural inheritance).</li>
+<li>Common has undergone significant simplification from Vrykul (inferred from the &ldquo;runt&rdquo; vrykul narrative and observed structural differences).</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>What is uncertain</h3>
+<ul>
+<li>Whether human names (<em>Thoradin</em>, <em>Arthas</em>, <em>Lothar</em>) contain Vrykul morphemes or are simply Common names.</li>
+<li>The extent of Vrykul vocabulary preservation in Common.</li>
+<li>Whether Common has a distinct grammar from English or is simply English within the Warcraft universe.</li>
+<li>The sound changes from Vrykul to Common.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>What is unknown</h3>
+<ul>
+<li>Common grammar and syntax (as distinct from English).</li>
+<li>Common vocabulary (as distinct from English).</li>
+<li>The degree of mutual intelligibility between Common and Vrykul.</li>
+<li>The timeline of Vrykul&rarr;Common divergence.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="references">References</h2>
+
+<ul>
+<li><em>World of Warcraft: Chronicle Volume 1</em> (2016). Blizzard Entertainment. (Human origins from vrykul; Tirisfal etymology.)</li>
+<li>Warcraft Wiki: Common (language) (warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/Common_(language))</li>
+<li>Warcraft Wiki: Human (warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/Human)</li>
+<li>Warcraft Wiki: Vrykul (warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/Vrykul)</li>
+<li>In-game: Multiple zones, cities, and NPC dialogue (all rendered in English/Common)</li>
+</ul>
+
