+<h3>Set 9: Failing/collapse root <span class="cog">*sindu</span></h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td class="reconstructed">*sindu</td>
+  <td>Sindu</td><td>Shindu</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>failing / falling / collapse</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td></td>
+  <td>Sindu-fallah-nor</td><td>Shindu fallah na</td><td></td><td></td><td>(both co-occur with <em>fallah/falah</em>)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td></td>
+  <td></td><td>Shindu sin'dorei</td><td></td><td></td><td>"Failing children of the blood"</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> Darnassian <em>Sindu</em> /ˈsɪndu/ (in <em>Sindu-fallah-nor</em>,
+uncertain gloss — possibly "sleep" or "fallen") and Thalassian <em>Shindu</em> /ˈʃɪndu/
+("failing/breaking" in <em>Shindu fallah na</em>, <em>Shindu sin'dorei</em>) differ only in
+initial /s/ vs /ʃ/. This is consistent with a regular palatalization
+<span class="sound-change">*s &gt; ʃ / _i</span> in Thalassian — a typologically common sound change.
+Both forms co-occur with <em>fallah</em>/<em>falah</em>, strengthening the cognate hypothesis.
+If confirmed, this would represent the second regular sound change between Darnassian and
+Thalassian (after <span class="sound-change">*u &gt; e / _#</span> in <em>*andu</em> &gt; <em>ande</em>).
+However, the Darnassian gloss ("sleep/fallen") and Thalassian gloss ("failing/breaking") show
+semantic divergence that requires explanation — perhaps a shared root meaning "collapse/fall"
+with independent semantic specialization. <strong>Confidence: Low–Moderate.</strong>
+See <a href="thalassian/grammar.html">Thalassian grammar</a> Investigation 3 for full analysis.</p>
+
+<h3>Set 10: Forest/place root <span class="cog">*tha-</span> / <span class="cog">*thas</span></h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr>
+  <td class="reconstructed">*tha-</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">(no standalone form)</td><td>Thas</td><td class="uncertain">Thal- (in <em>Thalyssra</em>)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>forest / place / home</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td></td>
+  <td></td><td>Thas'alah</td><td></td><td></td><td>"Light of the Forest"</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td></td>
+  <td></td><td>Thas'dorah</td><td></td><td></td><td>"Valor of the Forest"</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td></td>
+  <td></td><td>Thalas (in <em>Quel'Thalas</em>)</td><td></td><td></td><td>"Home / Kingdom"</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> Thalassian <em>Thas</em> ("forest") and <em>Thalas</em> ("home/kingdom")
+share the initial consonant cluster /θɑ/. If <em>Thalas</em> decomposes as <em>Tha-</em> + <em>-las</em>,
+then <em>Thas</em> could represent a reduced form of the same root via apocope. The semantic
+proximity ("forest" and "home") is suggestive for a people whose homeland is defined by its
+forests, but the connection is unconfirmed. The Shalassian proper name <em>Thalyssra</em>
+may contain the same <em>Thal-</em> element, though this is uncertain. <strong>Confidence: Low.</strong>
+See <a href="thalassian/grammar.html">Thalassian grammar</a> Investigation 3 for full analysis.</p>
+
+<h3>Set 11: Kinship suffix <span class="cog">*-da</span> / <span class="cog">*-na</span></h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr>
+  <td class="uncertain">(uncertain)</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>Ann'da, Minn'da</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>parent (father, mother)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td class="uncertain">(uncertain)</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>Shal'na</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>aunt / collateral relative</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> Thalassian is the only elven language with attested kinship terms.
+The suffix <em>-da</em> appears in both parent terms (<em>Ann'da</em> "papa", <em>Minn'da</em> "mama")
+while <em>-na</em> marks the aunt (<em>Shal'na</em>). This may reflect a grammaticalized
+kinship suffix system (parent vs. collateral relative), or it may be baby-talk (the universal
+/da/ pattern for parent terms). Without Darnassian, Shalassian, or Nazja kinship data,
+the Proto-Elven reconstruction is highly uncertain. Notably, the <em>Shal'</em> element in
+<em>Shal'na</em> is phonologically identical to the Shalassian root <em>Shal</em> ("twilight/arcane")
+— if cognate, this would be a rare triple elven cognate for a non-ethnonym word.
+<strong>Confidence: Low–Moderate</strong> for <em>-da</em> as kinship marker.
+See <a href="thalassian/grammar.html">Thalassian grammar</a> Investigation 1 for full analysis.</p>
