+<h3>Set 12: Definite article / subject marker <span class="cog">*anu</span></h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td class="reconstructed">*anu</td>
+  <td>Anu</td><td>Anu</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>the / definite article</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td></td>
+  <td><em>Anu'dora!</em>, <em>Anu Dorah</em>, <em>Anu dorini ash'ka</em>, <em>Anu therador mali</em>, <em>Anu'dorin</em></td><td><em>Anu belore dela'na</em> ("The sun will guide us")</td><td></td><td></td><td>(sentence-initial marker)</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> Thalassian <em>Anu</em> is confirmed as the definite article ("the") in
+<em>Anu belore dela'na</em> ("The sun will guide us"). The same element <em>Anu</em> appears in
+five Darnassian untranslated phrases, always sentence-initial, always preceding a noun phrase —
+exactly the syntactic slot where a definite article would appear. If Darnassian <em>Anu</em> is
+cognate with Thalassian <em>Anu</em>, this would be a shared <strong>grammatical morpheme</strong>
+inherited from Proto-Elven, not a Thalassian innovation. This is significant because shared
+grammatical morphemes are stronger evidence of genetic relationship than shared lexical items.
+<strong>Confidence: Moderate.</strong></p>
+
+<h3>Set 13: Death root <span class="cog">*tal</span> / <span class="cog">*talah</span></h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td class="reconstructed">*tal</td>
+  <td class="uncertain">(no standalone form)</td><td>Tal</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>death</td>
+</tr>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td class="reconstructed">*talah</td>
+  <td><em>Ishnu-talah</em> Kaldorei</td><td><em>talah</em> (in <em>Bash'a no falor talah!</em>), <em>A'Talah</em></td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>death (extended form)</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> Thalassian <em>Tal</em> ("death") is confirmed in <em>Tal anu'men no Sin'dorei</em>
+("Death to all who oppose the children of the blood"). The extended form <em>talah</em> appears in both
+languages: Thalassian <em>Bash'a no falor talah!</em> ("Taste the chill of true death!") and Darnassian
+<em>Ishnu-talah Kaldorei</em> (Lothrius Mooncaller). If <em>talah</em> = <em>tal</em> + <em>-ah</em>,
+the suffix <em>-ah</em> may be a shared derivational morpheme (nominalizer, verbalizer, or intensifier).
+In Darnassian <em>Ishnu-talah</em>, if <em>Ishnu</em> = "good fortune/blessings" and <em>talah</em> = "death,"
+the phrase means "Fortune in death, Kaldorei" — a warrior's farewell, semantically parallel to Thalassian
+<em>Bash'a no falor talah</em> ("Taste the chill of true death"). The <em>A'Talah Adore</em> on Vereesa's
+copper coin could mean "Death be with you" (a dark mirror of <em>Elune-adore</em>), further supporting
+the cross-linguistic reading. <strong>Confidence: Moderate–High</strong> for <em>tal</em> = "death" in
+both languages; <strong>Low–Moderate</strong> for <em>-ah</em> as a shared derivational suffix.</p>
+
+<h3>Set 14: Elune root <span class="cog">*elu-</span> in Thalassian</h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td class="reconstructed">*elun</td>
+  <td>Elun- (in <em>Elune</em>, <em>Elun'dris</em>, <em>Elun'falah</em>)</td><td>Elu- (in <em>Elu'meniel</em>)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>Elun- (in <em>Elun'alor</em>)</td><td>Elune / moon deity</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> The Proto-Elven root <em>*elun</em> is securely attested in Darnassian
+(<em>Elune</em>) and Nazja (<em>Elun'alor</em>), but was previously undocumented in Thalassian.
+However, <em>Elu'meniel</em> in the Thalassian blessing <em>Elu'meniel mal alann</em> ("May peace
+calm your heart") could be a reduced form of <em>Elune</em> used as a blessing invocation — structurally
+parallel to Darnassian <em>Elune-adore</em> ("Elune be with you"): [Deity name] + [blessing phrase].
+If <em>Elu'</em> = reduced <em>Elune</em>, then <em>Elu'meniel mal alann</em> = "Elune [grant] peace
+calm your heart." The alternative analysis (<em>Elu'</em> = optative particle, unrelated to Elune)
+cannot be ruled out, but the structural parallel with <em>Elune-adore</em> is compelling.
+If confirmed, this extends the <em>elun</em> cognate from 2 languages to 3.
+<strong>Confidence: Moderate.</strong></p>
+
