+<h3>Correspondence 6: Geminate simplification <span class="sound-change">*nn &gt; n / V_V</span> (Darnassian)</h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr><th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Evidence</th></tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr>
+  <td class="reconstructed">*annV-da</td>
+  <td><em>An'da</em> (single /n/)</td>
+  <td><em>Ann'da</em> (geminate /nn/)</td>
+  <td>Papa/Father — Darnassian simplifies intervocalic geminate</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td class="reconstructed">*minnV-da</td>
+  <td><em>Min'da</em> (single /n/)</td>
+  <td><em>Minn'da</em> (geminate /nn/)</td>
+  <td>Mama — identical pattern</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Inference:</strong> Darnassian systematically simplifies intervocalic geminate /nn/ to
+single /n/, while Thalassian retains the geminate. This contradicts the general expectation that
+the older language (Darnassian) would be more conservative — suggesting either that the gemination
+is a Thalassian innovation (expressive lengthening or assimilation), or that Proto-Elven had single
+/n/ and both languages innovated independently. With only 2 examples, the pattern is perfectly
+regular but the direction of change is uncertain. <strong>Confidence: Low–Moderate</strong>
+— only 2 cognate pairs show the pattern.</p>
+
+<h3>Correspondence 7: Shared <em>-ah</em> derivational suffix</h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr><th>Base root</th><th>+ <em>-ah</em> form</th><th>Language(s)</th><th>Gloss</th></tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr>
+  <td><em>tal</em> (death)</td>
+  <td><em>talah</em></td>
+  <td>Both</td>
+  <td>Death (extended form)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td><em>al</em> (light base)</td>
+  <td><em>alah</em></td>
+  <td>Both</td>
+  <td>Light</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td><em>fal</em> (balance/break base)</td>
+  <td><em>falah/fallah</em></td>
+  <td>Both</td>
+  <td>Balance / breaking</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Inference:</strong> The recurrent <em>-ah</em> /-ɑ/ ending on content words in both
+languages may be a productive derivational suffix (nominalizer, verbalizer, or intensifier) rather
+than merely a frequent word-final vowel. If <em>-ah</em> is a genuine morpheme, it would explain
+why <em>tal</em> "death" has an extended form <em>talah</em> in both languages. However, the high
+frequency of word-final /a/ in both languages makes it difficult to distinguish a true suffix from
+a phonological pattern. <strong>Confidence: Low</strong> — the pattern is suggestive but could be
+coincidental given the frequency of /a/ in elven word-final position.</p>
