+
+<div class="note">
+<strong>Caveat:</strong> The nerubian queen <em>Neferess</em> begins with <em>Nef-</em>, which
+could be <em>Ne-</em> + <em>-fer-</em> + <em>-ess</em>. However, <em>Neferess</em> is almost
+certainly Egyptian-inspired (cf. Egyptian <em>nfr</em> = "beautiful/good"), as is the Tol'vir
+<em>Neferset</em>. The similarity between <em>Ne-</em> in <em>Nezar'Azret</em> and <em>Nef-</em>
+in <em>Neferess</em> could reflect either the *N'-* prefix or shared Blizzard Egyptian design
+aesthetic &mdash; these hypotheses are difficult to disentangle.
+<br><br>
+<strong>Confidence: Very Low&ndash;Low</strong> for *N'-* as a cross-language Aqir morpheme.
+The naga <em>Nazj-</em> = <em>N-</em> + <em>Azj-</em> decomposition is the strongest
+evidence, but it remains unconfirmed. The nerubian <em>Nezar'Azret</em> evidence is weak
+due to the Egyptian design aesthetic confound.
+</div>
+
 <h3>The *-naxx* / *-nax* Suffix: Regular Sound Change?</h3>
 
