+<h3>Speculative Cognate Candidates (Sets 15–18)</h3>
+
+<p>The following pairs are identified as potential cognates but lack sufficient evidence
+for confirmation. They are documented here to facilitate future testing as the corpus grows.
+All carry Low–Moderate confidence.</p>
+
+<h3>Set 15: Root <span class="cog">*falah/*fallah</span> — "balance" / "breaking through"</h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td class="reconstructed">*falVh</td>
+  <td>falah (in <em>Andu-falah-dor</em> "Let balance be restored")</td><td>fallah (in <em>Shindu fallah na</em> "They are breaking through"; also <em>Sindu-fallah-nor</em>)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>balance ↔ breaking</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> The phonological form is nearly identical: Darnassian <em>falah</em>
+vs. Thalassian <em>fallah</em>, differing only in gemination of /l/. The semantic divergence
+is the key puzzle: Darnassian <em>falah</em> = "balance" (in a restoration context) while
+Thalassian <em>fallah</em> = "breaking through." A unified root meaning "disruption/overturning"
+could yield "balance" via semantic shift (the <em>restored</em> state after disruption) and
+"breaking through" via preservation of the active sense. This kind of semantic bifurcation
+from a root meaning "disruption" is typologically plausible (cf. English <em>revolution</em>:
+both "overturning" and "restored order"). The gemination in Thalassian <em>fallah</em> may
+reflect expressive lengthening or a different morphological decomposition (fal + <em>-lah</em>
+vs. fal + <em>-ah</em>). The root also co-occurs with <em>sindu/shindu</em> in both languages
+(<em>Sindu-fallah-nor</em> and <em>Shindu fallah na</em>), strengthening the cognate hypothesis
+through shared syntagmatic context. <strong>Confidence: Low–Moderate</strong> — phonologically
+strong but semantically divergent.</p>
+
+<h3>Set 16: Root <span class="cog">*dal</span> — "fortune / goodness"</h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td class="reconstructed">*dal</td>
+  <td>dal (in <em>Ishnu-dal-die</em> "Good fortune to you")</td><td>Dal (uncertain; possibly "fortune" or "goodness")</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>fortune / goodness</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> The element <em>dal</em> appears in Darnassian <em>Ishnu-dal-die</em>
+("Good fortune to you / farewell variant," Warcraft III voice line), where the context
+strongly suggests "fortune" or "goodness." Thalassian <em>Dal</em> is listed in the vocabulary
+with the same proposed meaning, and the Thalassian entry explicitly cross-references
+<em>Ishnu-dal-die</em> as a possible cross-language attestation. If <em>Ishnu-dal-die</em>
+is a shared elven farewell formula, then <em>dal</em> would be a third confirmed shared
+vocabulary item in that phrase alone (<em>Ishnu</em> and <em>-die</em> being the others).
+However, <em>dal</em> is a short CVC form with common consonants, and the meaning
+"fortune/goodness" is uncertain in both languages — it could be a different morpheme in
+each. The Thalassian greeting <em>Al diel shala</em> may contain <em>diel</em> related to
+<em>dal</em> (via vowel alternation /a/ ↔ /i/ + epenthetic /l/), but this is speculative.
+<strong>Confidence: Low–Moderate</strong> — same form and meaning in both languages, but
+short word and uncertain gloss.</p>
+
+<h3>Set 17: Root <span class="cog">*shan</span> — "honored" / unknown</h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td class="reconstructed">*shan</td>
+  <td>shan (in <em>Shan'do</em> "Honored Teacher"; <em>Thero'shan</em> "Beloved Student")</td><td>shan (in <em>Shan'dor</em> runestone; <em>Shan'dorah</em> Ren'dorei camp; untranslated <em>shanal</em>, <em>shan're</em>)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>Shan (personal name element)</td><td>honored / unknown</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> Darnassian <em>shan</em> appears in the confirmed title
+<em>Shan'do</em> ("Honored Teacher") with a gloss of "honored" or "learner" depending on
+position. Thalassian <em>shan</em> appears in proper nouns (<em>Shan'dor</em>, <em>Shan'dorah</em>)
+and untranslated phrases (<em>Balamore shanal!</em>, <em>da shan're</em>), but none of these
+have confirmed translations. The phonological identity is exact, and the appearance in
+apostrophe-marked compounds in both languages (<em>Shan'do</em>, <em>Shan'dor</em>) suggests
+a shared morphological pattern. Nazja also shows <em>Shan</em> as a personal name element,
+though this may be coincidental. The main obstacle is the lack of a confirmed gloss for
+Thalassian <em>shan</em> — without a translation, the cognate hypothesis rests on form
+alone. <strong>Confidence: Low</strong> — identical form and compounding pattern, but
+unconfirmed meaning in Thalassian.</p>
+
+<h3>Set 18: Root <span class="cog">*finel</span> — "the last"</h3>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+  <th>Proto-Elven</th><th>Darnassian</th><th>Thalassian</th><th>Shalassian</th><th>Nazja</th><th>Gloss</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr class="shared">
+  <td class="reconstructed">*finel</td>
+  <td>Finel /fɪˈnɛl/ ("the last," confirmed in Elegy novel)</td><td>finel (in <em>Endala finel endal!</em>, Nethermancer Sepethrea)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td>the last</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Analysis:</strong> Darnassian <em>Finel</em> is confirmed as "the last" from the
+novel <em>Elegy</em>. The same phonological form appears in the Thalassian combat phrase
+<em>Endala finel endal!</em> (Nethermancer Sepethrea), though this phrase is untranslated.
+If <em>finel</em> means "the last" in both languages, this would be a cognate with stable
+semantics — a rarer and more significant type than those showing semantic divergence.
+The form is phonologically distinctive (two syllables, medial /n/, final /ɛl/), making
+coincidental similarity less likely than for shorter morphemes. The phrase structure
+<em>Endala finel endal!</em> suggests <em>finel</em> may be a noun or adjective between
+two related elements (<em>Endala</em> and <em>endal</em>, possibly the same root with
+different suffixes), which could be consistent with "the last" as an attribute or title.
+However, without a confirmed Thalassian gloss, this remains speculative.
+<strong>Confidence: Low–Moderate</strong> — distinctive phonological form, confirmed Darnassian
+gloss, but unconfirmed Thalassian meaning.</p>
