-<h3>The <em>Andu</em> / <em>Ande</em> Correspondence</h3>
+<h3>The <em>Andu</em> / <em>Ande</em> / <em>Bandu</em> Correspondence</h3>
 
-<p>A particularly interesting pair is Darnassian <em>Andu</em> (in <em>Andu-falah-dor</em>,
-"Let balance be restored") and Thalassian <em>Ande</em> (in <em>Ande'thoras-ethil</em>,
-"May your troubles be diminished").</p>
+<p>A particularly interesting set of forms spans Darnassian and Thalassian, unified by the core
+element <em>-ndV</em>:</p>
 
 <table>
 <thead>
-<tr><th>Language</th><th>Form</th><th>Function</th><th>Phonological shape</th></tr>
+<tr><th>Language</th><th>Form</th><th>Function</th><th>Phonological Shape</th><th>Attested In</th></tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody>
 <tr>
-  <td>Darnassian</td><td><em>Andu</em></td><td>jussive/imperative particle</td><td>CV.CV</td>
+  <td>Darnassian</td><td><em>Andu</em></td><td>jussive ("let")</td><td>/ˈɑndu/</td><td><em>Andu-falah-dor</em></td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
-  <td>Thalassian</td><td><em>Ande</em></td><td>optative particle</td><td>CV.CV</td>
+  <td>Thalassian</td><td><em>Ande</em></td><td>optative ("may")</td><td>/ˈɑndɛ/</td><td><em>Ande'thoras-ethil</em></td>
 </tr>
+<tr>
+  <td>Darnassian</td><td><em>Bandu</em></td><td>imperative ("prepare")</td><td>/ˈbɑndu/</td><td><em>Bandu Thoribas</em></td>
+</tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 
-<p>The vowel alternation <em>u</em> ~ <em>e</em> may reflect:</p>
+<p>All three forms share the consonantal core <em>-nd-</em> with a following high vowel. The
+relationships suggest a paradigm:</p>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr><th></th><th>Jussive ("let")</th><th>Optative ("may")</th><th>Imperative ("prepare/do")</th></tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td><strong>Darnassian</strong></td><td><em>A-ndu</em></td><td><em>A-nde</em></td><td><em>Ba-ndu</em></td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>Thalassian</strong></td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td><td><em>A-nde</em></td><td class="uncertain">(not attested)</td></tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p>Three hypotheses explain the relationships:</p>
 <ol>
-  <li>A Proto-Elven <span class="sound-change">*andV-</span> with vowel gradation conditioned by
-      the following syllable (ablaut)</li>
-  <li>A semantic分化: <em>andu</em> = jussive ("let"), <em>ande</em> = optative ("may")</li>
-  <li>An independent sound change: Proto-Elven <span class="sound-change">*andu</span> &gt;
-      Thalassian <em>ande</em> via <span class="sound-change">u &gt; e / _CV</span>
-      (word-final vowel raising/fronting)</li>
+  <li><strong>Vowel gradation marks mood:</strong> <em>-ndu</em> = jussive/imperative mood,
+      <em>-nde</em> = optative mood. The vowel alternation /u/ ~ /e/ in the second syllable
+      signals a semantic distinction between "let/do" and "may/wish." This parallels the
+      Darnassian <em>-da</em>/ <em>-do</em> register alternation in kinship terms.</li>
+  <li><strong>Prefix marks person or emphasis:</strong> <em>A-</em> = third-person/unmarked,
+      <em>Ba-</em> = second-person/emphatic. The <em>Bandu</em> form would then be the
+      second-person imperative ("You prepare!"), while <em>Andu</em> is the third-person
+      jussive ("Let [it] be").</li>
+  <li><strong><em>Bandu</em> is unrelated to <em>Andu</em>:</strong> The similarity of
+      <em>-ndu</em> could be coincidental, and <em>Bandu</em> may be a distinct imperative
+      verb unrelated to the jussive particle.</li>
 </ol>
 
-<p>Hypothesis 3 is the most parsimonious. If correct, it represents the only attested
-regular sound change between Darnassian and Thalassian: <strong>word-final
-<span class="sound-change">*u &gt; e</span> in Thalassian</strong>. However, this rests on
-a single cognate pair and cannot be confirmed.</p>
+<p>If Hypotheses 1 and 2 are both correct, Darnassian would have a systematic imperative paradigm
+with prefix (<em>A-</em>/<em>Ba-</em>) marking person and vowel (<em>-u</em>/<em>-e</em>) marking
+mood. This would be the most grammatically rich subsystem identified in Darnassian, but the
+evidence remains extremely limited (3 forms total).</p>
 
+<p>The <em>Andu</em> &rarr; <em>Ande</em> change specifically (Darnassian to Thalassian) may also
+reflect a regular sound change: <strong>word-final <span class="sound-change">*u &gt; e</span>
+in Thalassian</strong>. If correct, it would be the only attested regular sound change between
+the two languages. However, this rests on a single cognate pair and cannot be confirmed.</p>
+
