 <p><strong>Overall assessment:</strong> The strongest case for genetic relationship comes from the <strong>convergence of multiple weak signals</strong> rather than any single strong cognate. Three independent morphemes (*-da*, *ala-*, *na*) with Low–Moderate confidence each provide a pattern consistent with deep genetic relationship at ~16,000 years of divergence. The *Ma'/Min'* correspondence is now assessed as likely irregular (not a regular sound change), as counterexamples exist (*na* and *-da* both have /a/ in both families). No single cognate is conclusive, but the accumulation of shared features is more than would be expected by chance alone.</p>
 
 <hr>
-<nav style="text-align:center;margin:1em 0;">
+<h2 id="harani">Deeply Divergent Branch: Hara'ni</h2>
+
+<p>The <a href="haranir/grammar.html">Hara'ni</a> language, spoken by the Haranir (ancient subterranean bat-folk), is confirmed to descend from dark trolls/night elves (<em>The Art of Midnight</em>, pg. 125): "those who would eventually become the haranir split from them and journeyed deep below the ground." This makes Hara'ni a <strong>deeply divergent branch of the proto-troll/elven language family</strong>, separated by ~16,000 years of independent development. While 16,000 years typically erases cognate recognition beyond the most deeply entrenched morphemes, four potential cognate candidates have been identified through systematic comparison. Full analysis in <a href="darnassian/grammar.html">Darnassian grammar</a> Investigation 13.</p>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr><th>#</th><th>Hara'ni</th><th>Elven/Zandali</th><th>Proposed Proto-Form</th><th>Semantic Match</th><th>Confidence</th><th>Key Issue</th></tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr>
+  <td>H1</td>
+  <td><em>-nir</em> "people" (collective ethnonym; <em>Haranir</em>)</td>
+  <td><em>-anir</em>/<em>-nir</em> (Darnassian: <em>Alash'anir</em>, <em>Shala'nir</em>, <em>Daral'nir</em>)</td>
+  <td class="reconstructed">*-nir</td>
+  <td>collective/agentive suffix ↔ collective/agentive suffix</td>
+  <td>Low&ndash;Moderate</td>
+  <td>Exact phonological match; semantic gap ("people" vs. "bringer/bearer"); small sample</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td>H2</td>
+  <td><em>Har'</em> "village" (4 confirmed attestations)</td>
+  <td><em>Aran</em>/<em>-naar</em> "town/settlement" (Darnassian: 7 attestations)</td>
+  <td class="reconstructed">*harVn</td>
+  <td>village ↔ town/settlement (exact)</td>
+  <td>Low&ndash;Moderate</td>
+  <td>Exact semantic match; requires initial /h/ loss in Darnassian (typologically common but not independently confirmed)</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td>H3</td>
+  <td><em>Aln-</em> "deep/source/essence" (4 attestations)</td>
+  <td><em>*ala-</em> "bright/burning/essence" (Zandali <em>Alai</em>, elven <em>alah</em>)</td>
+  <td class="reconstructed">*ala-</td>
+  <td>source/essence ↔ fire/light (divergent from "potency/essence")</td>
+  <td>Very Low&ndash;Low</td>
+  <td>Extra *-n* in Hara'ni unexplained; semantic gap significant; coherent only if proto-meaning was "essence/potency"</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+  <td>H4</td>
+  <td><em>-mara</em> "balance" (confirmed in <em>Har'mara</em>)</td>
+  <td><em>-mara</em> (Zandali: <em>Shaol'mara</em>, <em>Kul'amara</em>; no confirmed gloss)</td>
+  <td class="reconstructed">*-mara</td>
+  <td>balance ↔ unknown</td>
+  <td>Very Low</td>
+  <td>Phonologically exact but no Zandali semantic evidence; /mara/ is a common syllable</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p><strong>Overall assessment:</strong> The convergence of multiple weak signals is consistent with deep genetic relationship at ~16,000 years. The strongest individual candidates are <em>-nir</em> (phonologically exact suffix with consistent morphological function) and <em>Har'/Aran</em> (semantically exact with a typologically natural sound change). If additional cognate pairs sharing the proposed /h/ &rarr; &empty; correspondence are discovered, this would independently validate the <em>Har'/Aran</em> analysis. No single candidate is conclusive, but the pattern exceeds chance expectations for languages separated by 16,000 years.</p>
