+
+  <li><strong>Stress assignment by word length</strong>: Disyllabic words show consistent initial (trochaic) stress (<em>Alah</em>, <em>Anar</em>, <em>Shindu</em>, <em>Felo</em>, <em>Fallah</em>, <em>Ethil</em>, <em>Shala</em>, <em>Anu</em>, <em>Ande</em>, <em>Dorei</em>), while trisyllabic and longer words show penultimate stress (<em>Belore</em> be-LO-re, <em>Selama</em> se-LA-ma, <em>Arkhana</em> ar-KHA-na, <em>Malanorei</em> ma-la-NO-rei). The sole exception is <em>Alann</em> (a-LANN), where the heavy CVC coda of the final syllable attracts stress. This trochaic-to-penult shift by word length is consistent across the attested corpus.</li>
