 <p>The phonological coincidence analysis is weakened by the observation that the <em>-ah</em> forms are morphologically paired with base forms lacking <em>-ah</em> (<em>tal</em>/<em>talah</em>, <em>al</em>/<em>alah</em>, <em>fal</em>/<em>falah</em>). If word-final /-ah/ were purely accidental, we would not expect it to systematically correspond to shorter cognate forms.</p>
 
 <p><strong>Confidence: Low&ndash;Moderate</strong> for <em>-ah</em> as a Proto-Elven derivational suffix. The three independent cognate pairs are consistent with a unified analysis, and the semantic coherence (nominalizer/stative resultative) is typologically natural. However, the small sample (3 pairs) and the frequency of /a/ in word-final position leave open the possibility of coincidence. If additional <em>-ah</em> pairs are identified (e.g., a root <em>X</em> with both <em>X</em> and <em>Xah</em> attested), confidence would increase significantly.</p>
 
+<h3>Investigation 15: The <em>Thas</em> Toponymic Morpheme &mdash; Cross-Elven &ldquo;Forest&rdquo; Element</h3>
+
+<p>The expanded Darnassian place name corpus reveals <em>Thas</em> as a toponymic morpheme meaning &ldquo;forest/woodland&rdquo;, previously attested only in Thalassian:</p>
+
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr><th>Language</th><th>Attestation</th><th>Context</th><th>Gloss</th></tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td>Darnassian</td><td><em>Thas'talah</em></td><td>Night elven glade</td><td><em>Thas</em> = &ldquo;forest&rdquo; + <em>'talah</em> (unknown)</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Thalassian</td><td><em>Thas'alah</em></td><td>&ldquo;Light of the Forest&rdquo;</td><td><em>Thas</em> = &ldquo;forest&rdquo; + <em>'alah</em> = &ldquo;light&rdquo;</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Thalassian</td><td><em>Thas'dorah</em></td><td>&ldquo;Valor of the Forest&rdquo;</td><td><em>Thas</em> = &ldquo;forest&rdquo; + <em>'dorah</em> = &ldquo;valor&rdquo;</td></tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+
+<p>If <em>Thas</em> is genuinely Darnassian (not a Thalassian borrowing), it would confirm the morpheme as Proto-Elven rather than a Thalassian innovation. The name <em>Thas'talah</em> appears in an exclusively night elven context (no blood elf association), supporting native Darnassian usage.</p>
+
+<p>The relationship between <em>Thas</em> &ldquo;forest&rdquo; and <em>Thalas</em> &ldquo;home/realm&rdquo; is noteworthy: both begin with /&theta;/ + vowel + alveolar consonant + vowel + sibilant. Three hypotheses:</p>
+
+<ol>
+<li><strong>Cognate roots</strong>: <em>Thas</em> and <em>Thalas</em> share a Proto-Elven root <em>thVs-</em> meaning &ldquo;place/territory&rdquo;, which specialized to &ldquo;forest&rdquo; in one branch and &ldquo;home/realm&rdquo; in another</li>
+<li><strong>Thas as compound element</strong>: <em>Thas</em> = <em>Thal</em> + <em>-as</em> where <em>Thal</em> relates to <em>Thalas</em> and <em>-as</em> is an unknown suffix</li>
+<li><strong>Coincidental similarity</strong>: The phonological similarity is accidental; <em>Thas</em> &ldquo;forest&rdquo; and <em>Thalas</em> &ldquo;home&rdquo; are unrelated</li>
+</ol>
+
+<p><strong>Confidence: Low&ndash;Moderate</strong> for <em>Thas</em> as a Darnassian toponymic morpheme. The Darnassian attestation (<em>Thas'talah</em>) is a single example, and the morpheme could be a Thalassian borrowing rather than a native cognate. However, the Thalassian evidence (2 independent compounds) is strong, and the cross-elven distribution would be consistent with Proto-Elven inheritance.</p>
+
