 5. **Palato-alveolar consonants**: *Shul'ka* (/ʃ/), *Chonon* (/tʃ/), *Or'jan* (/dʒ/), *Zhakir* (/ʒ/) introduce a new series of palato-alveolar consonants not previously attested. These may reflect a phonological subsystem used primarily in compound-forming elements or religious vocabulary.
 
 6. **Dental fricative /θ/**: *Har'athir* and *Dreth'amar* introduce /θ/ (th-sound), a relatively rare phoneme in WoW constructed languages. In Hara'ni, /θ/ appears in compound suffixes.
 
+9. **Open final syllable preference**: Hara'ni personal names overwhelmingly end in open syllables — final vowels /a/, /i/, /u/: /a/-final (*Orweyna*, *Gnarla*, *Mowaia*, *Tawroa*, *Yaila*, *Shay'neia*, *Ney'leia*, *Ruia*, *Ah'wara*, *Amwa'ana*, *Har'mara*, *Ahl'ua*, *Na'bala*, *Ka'dani*), /i/-final (*Kalni*, *Kamari*, *Hawli*, *Habissi*, *Ruutani*, *Hara'ni*, *Kai'shae*, *Ir'ayi*), /u/-final (*Maku*, *Chua*, *Zo'ya*). Consonant-final exceptions (*Hagar*, *Hannan*, *Amarakk*, *Manat*) are few. The ratio is approximately 20+ open-syllable names to 4 closed-syllable names — the strongest open-syllable preference of any WoW constructed language. This may be a Hara'ni innovation developed during 16,000 years of subterranean isolation, since neither Darnassian nor Zandali shows this degree of open-syllable fixation.
+
+10. **Pre-apostrophe closed syllable, post-apostrophe open onset asymmetry**: A striking syllable-structure asymmetry exists across the apostrophe boundary in Hara'ni compounds. Pre-apostrophe elements overwhelmingly end in a consonant (closed syllable): *Ter'* (/tɛr/), *Ran'* (/ræn/), *Har'* (/hɑːr/), *Aln'* (/æln/), *Shul'* (/ʃʊl/), *Zur'* (/zʊr/), *En'* (/ɛn/), *Ul'* (/ʊl/). Post-apostrophe elements overwhelmingly begin with a consonant + vowel (open onset): *-kova*, *-hune*, *-rangi*, *-layro*, *-alnor*, *-athir*, *-ka*, *-ashar*, *-dani*. The pattern is: [closed syllable]'[open onset + ...]. This means the apostrophe falls at a natural syllable boundary — after a coda consonant and before an onset consonant — and is phonotactically natural (avoids hiatus) across ~25 compound names.
+
+11. **Intervocalic /h/ preservation at morpheme boundaries**: /h/ is conspicuously preserved at apostrophe boundaries where the second element begins with /h/: *Ran'hune*, *On'hiea*, *Aln'hara*. In many WoW languages, /h/ at morpheme boundaries would be elided or reduced. Hara'ni preserves it, suggesting that the glottal fricative serves a boundary-marking function — reinforcing the morpheme division that the apostrophe indicates orthographically. This connects to the high /h/ frequency already documented: /h/ is not just frequent initially but is specifically maintained at structurally significant positions.
+
+12. **Diphthong rarity — divergence from elven heritage**: Despite being genetically related to the elven/troll language family, Hara'ni names show remarkably few diphthongs: only ~4 names out of 30+ (*Nye'rangi* /aɪ/, *Kai'shae* /aɪ/, *Orweyna* /eɪ/, *Har'kuai* /aɪ/). The overwhelming majority of Hara'ni vowel sequences are monophthongs. This represents a significant phonological divergence from their elven/troll heritage over 16,000 years, suggesting either diphthong simplification (monophthongization) in the subterranean environment or independent development. The rarity of diphthongs also aligns with the Xhosa phonological parallel — Xhosa has relatively few diphthongs.
