+<p><strong>Overall assessment:</strong> The strongest case for genetic relationship comes from the <strong>convergence of multiple weak signals</strong> rather than any single strong cognate. Four independent morphemes (*-da*, *ala-*, *na*, *no*) with Low–Moderate confidence each provide a pattern consistent with deep genetic relationship at ~16,000 years of divergence. The *no* connective in loa names is particularly notable: Zandali *no* (dedicatory "to/for" in ritual contexts) and Thalassian *No* ("to/all") may reflect a shared proto-form *\*no* that split functionally — *na* retained general spatial/relational functions while *no* specialized for dedicatory/ritual use (see <a href="zandali/grammar.html#investigation6b">Zandali grammar Investigation 6B</a>). The *Ma'/Min'* correspondence (entry 4) is now assessed as likely irregular (not a regular sound change), as counterexamples exist (*na* and *-da* both have /a/ in both families). No single cognate is conclusive, but the accumulation of shared features — now including *no* as a fifth independent signal — is more than would be expected by chance alone.</p>
 
